  "answer_text": "The most widely accepted and \"official\" way to pronounce **doge** is **\"dohj\"**. \n\nHere is a breakdown of how to say it, why it\u2019s pronounced that way, and the other common variations you might hear:\n\n### 1. The Official Pronunciation: **\"dohj\"** (or **\"dohzh\"**)\n* **How to say it:** It starts with a long \"o\" (like in the words *go*, *no*, or *vogue*) and ends with a soft \"j\" sound (like the \"dg\" in *badge* or *judge*). \n* **Sounds like:** **\"Doje\"** (think of the word \"dojo\" but drop the \"o\" at the end). \n* **The \"French\" variation:** Many people also pronounce it as **\"dohzh\"**, where the ending has a soft \"zh\" sound, like the \"g\" in *mirage*, *garage*, or *genre*. \n* **Phonetic spelling:** `/do\u028ad\u0292/` or `/do\u028a\u0292/`\n\n### Why is this the \"official\" way?\nThe meme\u2019s spelling and pronunciation originally come from a June 2005 episode of the puppet-show web series ***Homestar Runner***. In the episode, the character Homestar spells out \"D-O-G-E\" to Strong Bad, and Strong Bad replies by pronouncing it **\"dohj\"**. \n\nWhen Jackson Palmer and Billy Markus created **Dogecoin** in 2013, they explicitly stated they adopted the pronunciation from that *Homestar Runner* clip. Coincidentally, this also matches the pronunciation of the actual, centuries-old English dictionary word \"doge\" (which refers to the historical rulers of Venice and Genoa).\n\n---\n\n### Other Common (But Technically \"Incorrect\") Pronunciations\nBecause \"doge\" is an internet word, people guessed how to say it for years before a consensus was reached. You will still hear people say:\n\n1. **\"Dogue\" (rhymes with *vogue* or *rogue*):** This uses a long \"o\" but a hard \"g\" sound. People favor this because \"dog\" has a hard \"g,\" so they assume adding an \"e\" just makes the vowel long. \n2. **\"Doggie\":** Pronounced exactly like a puppy. \n3. **\"Doh-ghey\" / \"Do-geh\":** Pronouncing both syllables. Because the Shiba Inu in the meme (Kabosu) is Japanese, some people assumed the word was Japanese and pronounced it with a Japanese-style ending.\n4. **\"Dodge\" (rhymes with *lodge*):** Using a short \"o\" and a soft \"j\". \n\n### The Bottom Line\nIf you want to sound like you know the meme and cryptocurrency space, say **\"dohj\"** (rhymes with *vogue* but ends like *judge*). However, as the co-creators of Dogecoin have famously pointed out, the entire meme is meant to be silly and lighthearted\u2014so nobody is going to stop you if you prefer to say it another way!",
